Q: How to install and use TailwindCSS into StencilJS via PostCSS A: <p>If you don't want to rely on <code>@stencil-community/postcss</code>, you have the option to use the Tailwind CLI, which is a framework-independent interpreter.</p>
<p>The advantage, there are no compatibility compromises. The downside, however, is that it cannot be integrated into the Stencil startup process, so during development it must be run as a separate CSS build in a second terminal alongside Stencil. For production, the TailwindCSS build must be completed strictly before Stencil.</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>npm install tailwindcss @tailwindcss/cli --save-dev
</code></pre>
<p>It's important to provide StencilJS with the file generated by the Tailwind CLI in the configuration. The filename can be custom; <code>input.css</code> and <code>output.css</code> are used here just for clarity in the example.</p>
<p><strong>stencil.config.ts</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import { Config } from '@stencil/core';

export const config: Config = {
  namespace: 'app',
  globalStyle: 'src/global/output.css',
};
</code></pre>
<p>So you create <code>input.css</code>. Based on this, the Tailwind CLI will generate the corresponding <code>output.css</code>.</p>
<p><strong>./src/global/input.css</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@import &quot;tailwindcss&quot;;
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>npx @tailwindcss/cli -i ./src/global/input.css -o ./src/global/output.css --watch
</code></pre>
<p>While running this, start StencilJS in a separate terminal window and continue development (usually <code>npm run start</code> references to this):</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>stencil build --dev --watch --serve
</code></pre>
<p>References:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://tailwindcss.com/docs/installation/tailwind-cli" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://tailwindcss.com/docs/installation/tailwind-cli</a></li>
<li><a href="https://stenciljs.com/docs/styling#global-styles" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://stenciljs.com/docs/styling#global-styles</a></li>
</ul>
<h3>Build StencilJS with Tailwind CLI</h3>
<p>As I mentioned, always generate the TailwindCSS output CSS first (without <code>--watch</code> flag):</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>npx @tailwindcss/cli -i ./src/global/input.css -o ./src/global/output.css
</code></pre>
<p>After that, run the StencilJS build (usually <code>npm run build</code> references to this):</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>stencil build
</code></pre>
<h3>Extra: Using <code>@apply</code> in component-specific CSS</h3>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79877280/15167500">How to use TailwindCSS in component's CSS file</a></li>
</ul>
<p>With the difference that you must always reference <code>input.css</code>:</p>
<p><strong>package.json</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>{
  …
  &quot;imports&quot;: {
    &quot;#tailwindcss&quot;: &quot;./src/global/input.css&quot;
  },
  …
} 
</code></pre>
<p><strong>./src/app/your-component.css</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>@reference &quot;#tailwindcss&quot;;

.example {
  @apply bg-custom;
}
</code></pre>
